==English==

{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From the {{etyl|la|en}} ''[[instigatus]]'', past participle of {{term|instigare||to instigate}}, from prefix ''[[in-]]'' in + *{{term||stigare}}, akin to {{term|stinguere||push, goad}}. Akin to {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|stechen|lang=de||to prick}}, {{etyl|en|-}} [[stick]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-instigate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|instigat|ing}}

# To [[goad]] or [[urge]] forward; to [[set on]]; to [[provoke]]; to [[incite]].
#: ''He hath only '''instigated''' his blackest agents to the very extent of their malignity.''  -Bp. Warburton.

====Usage notes====
Commonly used with reference to evil actions; as, to instigate one to a crime.

====Synonyms====
* {{qualifier|to goad or urge forward}}: [[animate]], [[encourage]], [[impel]], [[incite]], [[provoke]], [[spur]], [[stimulate]], [[tempt]], [[urge]]

====Antonyms====
* {{qualifier|to goad or urge forward}}: [[halt]], [[prevent]], [[stop]]

